WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.160
Booting Linux from RAM is not one single feature.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:07.460
It is a family of designs where some part of the running system is backed by memory

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.840
instead of ordinary persistent storage.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.140
In one design, a live image is copied into RAM during boot,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.680
so the system can run with less dependence on the USB drive, optical media,

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.820
or network source that started it.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.480
In another design, the base root filesystem is read-only,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.460
and a writable tmpfs overlay records session changes in RAM.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.640
In another,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.180
the initramfs stages early boot work in memory before the final root filesystem is

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.200
mounted.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.100
Some systems go further and make the whole root effectively volatile,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.560
so each reboot returns to a clean state. The appeal is practical.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.980
RAM-backed reads can be fast after the system is loaded. Disk writes can be reduced,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.760
which helps low-wear media and lab images.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.700
A live session can be portable. A kiosk, classroom, training machine,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.040
or recovery environment can reset cleanly after each boot.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.820
A test machine can be changed freely because the changes disappear unless they are

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.480
deliberately saved.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.780
The warning is just as important: RAM is temporary. If the system reboots, loses power,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.080
crashes, or runs out of memory, RAM-only changes are gone.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.020
That can be a feature when the goal is clean rollback,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.120
but it can be a disaster if the learner expected logs, configuration, updates,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.340
or user data to persist.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.440
Good RAM-boot design starts with a clear goal. Are you trying to remove dependence on the

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.860
boot device after startup? Reduce writes to storage?

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.200
Build a disposable lab workstation? Keep a read-only base image with a temporary writable

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.220
layer? Test software in a clean environment?

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:49.640
Each goal points to a different pattern and a different persistence plan. Avoid treating

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.180
RAM booting as automatically faster,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.320
safer, or more private.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.620
It is an engineering choice with tradeoffs that must match the workload, hardware,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.160
distribution, and recovery plan.
